Python OOPs – Inheritance
📌 Phase 3: Inheritance
1. What is Inheritance?

Inheritance is an OOP concept where a child class can acquire the attributes and methods of a parent class.

It helps in code reuse and avoids writing the same code again.

2. Parent / Base Class

The class whose properties and methods are inherited is called the parent class or base class.

3. Child / Derived Class

The class that inherits from another class is called the child class or derived class.

🔹 Types of Inheritance
Single Inheritance

One child class inherits from one parent class.

Multiple Inheritance

One child class inherits from multiple parent classes.

Multilevel Inheritance

Inheritance occurs in multiple levels.

Parent → Child → Grandchild

Hierarchical Inheritance

Multiple child classes inherit from the same parent class.

Hybrid Inheritance

A combination of two or more types of inheritance.

🔄 Method Overriding

When a child class provides its own implementation of a method that already exists in the parent class, it is called method overriding.

🔼 super()

super() is used to access methods or the constructor of the parent class from the child class.

It is commonly used to reuse the parent's initialization or behavior.

🏗️ Constructor Inheritance

A child class can use the constructor of its parent class.

If the child defines its own __init__(), we can use super().__init__() to call the parent constructor.

🔍 Method Resolution Order (MRO)

MRO determines the order in which Python searches for methods and attributes when inheritance is involved.

We can check it using:

ClassName.mro()

or

ClassName.__mro__
🔢 C3 Linearization

C3 Linearization is the algorithm Python uses to calculate the MRO, especially when multiple inheritance is involved.

It ensures that Python follows a consistent and predictable order when searching for methods.

